  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1226 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a closed upper low tracking east through \neastern Nebraska. Meanwhile, weak ridging aloft is slowly amplifying \nacross the Desert Southwest. Near the surface, a very broad area of \nhigh pressure is drifting east through the Great Lakes.\n\nRelatively dry conditions are forecast early in the period as the \nSREF indicates weak ridging aloft transitioning slowly east through \nthe Southern Rockies tonight, and into the Western High Plains Tuesday.\nA stray thunderstorm or two cannot be ruled out in extreme southwest\nKansas late overnight/early Tuesday morning as a weak perturbation\nclimbing the backside of the approaching ridge axis interacts with\na near stationary frontal boundary in southeast Colorado. More \nappreciable rainfall chances (70-90%) arrive Wednesday night as the\naforementioned ridge shifts farther east into the Central Plains,\ngiving way to a slowly intensifying southwest flow aloft across the\nhigh plains of eastern Colorado/western Kansas. A prevailing south-\nsoutheasterly upslope flow will reinforce low level moisture with\ndewpoints pushing the lower 60s(F), providing sufficient instability.\nShower/thunderstorm development is likely across northeast New Mexico\ninto southeast Colorado late Wednesday afternoon/early Wednesday\nevening as an upper level shortwave lifts northeast across the Southern\nRockies, with storms then spreading northeast into southwest Kansas\novernight into early Thursday. The best chance for rainfall initially\nwill be across extreme southwest Kansas where the latest NBM paints\na widespread 50-70% probability of 12-hr QPF exceeding 0.25 of an\ninch by early Thursday morning with showers and storms moving farther\neast toward central Kansas later in the day Thursday.\n\nSeasonably cool temperatures are forecast again tonight with a broad \nsurface high drifting east through the Great Lakes allowing a easterly\nupslope flow to persist across the Western High Plains, reinforcing\nthe cooler air mass across the area. The latest HREF paints a \nwidespread 50-70% probability of temperatures slipping below 60F\nby daybreak Tuesday morning. With the help of some clearing skies\nearlier in the overnight period, look for lows generally in the\nmid/upper 50s(F) to near 60F. Similar temperatures to today can\nbe expected Tuesday with a slow to develop southeast upslope flow\ndoing little to erode the cooler air mass across the area, resulting\nin H85 temperatures holding around 15C in central/south central Kansas\nto the upper teens(C) out near the Colorado line. Expect afternoon\nhighs generally in the 70s(F) in west central and central Kansas where\nthe HREF indicates only a 40-60% probability of temperatures exceeding\n70F to a 70-90% probability of temperatures topping 75F near and\nalong the Oklahoma border. Similar temperatures are likely Wednesday.",
